# Components (Ink + React)

## Naming and exports

- File names are **kebab-case** and match the component: `finding-card-view.tsx` → `FindingCardView`.
  Hooks: `use-switching.tsx` → `useSwitching`. Views end in `-view`.
- **Named exports**, **arrow functions** for components and local helpers. No `export default`.
- One exported component per file. Small private pieces that only serve it (a row, a skeleton) stay in the
  same file.

## Props

- Extract props into a named type before the component. Use `Props` unless a file has several props types.
- Destructure in the parameter list.

```tsx
// good
type Props = {
  title: string;
  width: number;
  onClose: () => void;
};

export const HelpView = ({ title, width, onClose }: Props) => { ... };

// avoid: inline object type in the signature, or `props.x` all through the body
```

## Handlers

- Callbacks passed as props are `onSomething` (`onPick`, `onCancel`, `onDone`).
- Inside a component, name the function after what it does for the user (`checkFix`, `startReview`); it
  may be passed to an `onX` prop.
- `useInput` handlers dispatch, they do not contain logic: look the key up in a keymap, call a named action.

## Rendering

- Optional content: `{condition && <X />}`. Do not write `{condition ? <X /> : null}`. A ternary is for when
  both branches render something.
- No logic in JSX beyond that: compute values above the return and give them names.
- Rows of text are computed by pure functions (`buildStream`, `cardBody`) and rendered by the component; the
  component does not assemble strings by hand.

## State

- Minimise `useEffect` and `useState`. Prefer values derived during render (`useMemo` for the expensive
  ones) over state synchronised by an effect.
- Shared or domain state belongs to the slice's `model` and is read with `useSyncExternalStore`; local UI
  state (cursor, open menu, typed text) stays in the view.
- An effect that talks to the outside world cleans up after itself (unsubscribe, clear timers).

## Size

- A component file is under ~250 lines. When a view mixes layout, key handling and derived data, split it:
  keymap and actions into their own files, derived data into `features/<slice>/lib`, repeated blocks into
  `shared/ui`.
